## 1. What is fixed and what may change

Fixed (W7): every result and number (the `paper/numbers.tex` macros and the `results/` files
behind them), every verdict and its status (registered pass/fail, undetermined, post hoc,
retrospective, development, theory), the paper's conclusions, and the set of studies. No run, no
new analysis, no new statistic computed from raw runs, no new direction.

May change: the order and division of sections, paragraphs and sentences; wording and terminology;
which detail sits in the main text and which in the appendices; figure and table rendering and
layout drawn from values already in `results/` (W7); captions; the sentences around citations
and, where a sentence misdescribes a work, the citation itself (no work after 18 Oct 2026); the
strength or scope of a claim's wording, in either direction, to what the evidence shows (W6).

Decision rule. A change is in scope if it changes how a result is stated, ordered or shown. It is out
of scope if it changes a number, a verdict or its status, adds a claim no results file supports, or
needs a computation that is not already in the repositories. When the text and the evidence
disagree, the evidence wins and the text changes (W6); a number in the text that differs from its
results file is corrected, never the file. Whether the evidence supports a wording is
`experiment-repo`'s call (analysis); whether a change alters a conclusion is the plan's
(`story-repo`, asked in this repository); `paper-repo` writes the text. Every change to a claim's
strength or scope names its evidence in the change record (Section 8).

## 2. Reading of the delivered paper (the plan's diagnosis)

This is the plan's reading of the main text at 771f5a9, to direct the audits of Section 3. It is not
a review verdict; each owner confirms or rejects the points in its part. Locations are PDF pages.

### 2.1 Structure and order (W1)

- S1. Two papers in one: Secs. 3-6 (the race and its tests) and Sec. 7 (five extensions) each carry
  their own results summary; the abstract and the contribution list enumerate about twelve results
  with about twenty numbers, with no order of importance. The answer to the title question is not
  stated in one place.
- S2. Topics are split across sections: AdamW appears in Fig. 1 (grey points), Sec. 6.1 "Where it
  fails", Sec. 7.1, Sec. 7.3 (two-layer encoder) and Sec. 8; the ViT in Sec. 6.3 and Sec. 7.5; the
  two-layer encoder in Sec. 6.2 and Sec. 7.3; the layer-normalised target in Sec. 7.2 and Sec. 7.3.
- S3. Sec. 7.3 "A deep linear encoder" also carries the deep random predictor, the two-layer MLP
  encoder under gradient descent, under AdamW and under the normalised target (pp. 8-9): five
  results under one heading.
- S4. The picture of the mechanism (Fig. 3, drive against decay, App. A) is cited from Sec. 4.2 but
  lives in the appendix; the main text shows no picture of the race before Fig. 1 (p. 6).
- S5. Sec. 8 is one paragraph, mostly limitations; the conclusions and the paper's position on
  design (no schedule or predictor design is recommended) are scattered over Secs. 7.4, 8.
- S6. The appendix order (A proofs, B further limits, C aligned structure, D related work,
  E experimental details, F extension) does not follow the main text (Sec. 2 points to D, Sec. 3 to
  C), and appendices are cited as "Section A".

### 2.2 How claims are stated and supported (W2, W6)

- C1. Numbers appear before what they measure is said: the rank error (mean absolute error of
  the predicted rank over runs, bootstrap 95% interval) is defined in Sec. 6, after the abstract
  and contributions use it; "within one", "majority oracle", "precision", "sample-robust runs",
  "the count at T_h" are used without a definition at first use.
- C2. Sentences carry four to eight numbers (Secs. 7.1-7.5); the verdict of a test is hard to find,
  and the registered / post hoc / retrospective status is marked unevenly.
- C3. Process detail stands in for the claim: "A first frozen model (v3) ... with (iv) added (v4)",
  "known before the last scorer fix", "the word is undetermined (truth ill-posed)".
- C4. Places where the plan expects the claim-evidence audit to look first (not findings):
  (a) contribution 3's "the race predicts the epoch-50 critical weight decay" against Sec. 7.5's
  "right in direction and rough size only" and "not separated from E3's step-0 rule";
  (b) the scope words of the AdamW and normalised-target claims (aligned basis, full batch, linear
  encoder, fixed horizon) in the abstract, contributions and Sec. 8;
  (c) "the premise fails" (contributions) against Sec. 7.5 (raising decay: no cell; lowering:
  D = 10 and 20);
  (d) the general sentences of the introduction and conclusion ("no criterion predicts when a
  configuration collapses", "with weight decay collapse is an attractor, escaped by outgrowing
  the predictor's decay") against what the linear and ViT results show;
  (e) "Weight decay, not the predictor or target, sets Arm T's collapse" (Sec. 6.3);
  (f) "We recover these results as limits (Section D)" (Sec. 2).
  Statements the text makes weaker than the evidence count as much as stronger ones (W6).

### 2.3 Wording (W3)

- T1. Internal labels of the study plan in the main text: E1, E1b, E1c, E2, E3, A1, A2', N2, B1',
  B3, v3, v4, rung 1a-4, Arm T / Arm P, T_h, D, TOST, "word", "truth ill-posed", "tight / signed /
  gain-adapted mu count". A reader of the 9 pages cannot decode them.
- T2. Related baselines go by several names: "initial window", "stability count at
  initialisation", "mu count", "step-0 rule", "static bound", "static threshold".
- T3. Long sentences with nested parentheses and semicolon chains (abstract, contributions,
  Sec. 7).

### 2.4 Figures and tables (W4)

- F1. Fig. 2 joins three unrelated results (AdamW count, deep-encoder reading time, ViT switch);
  its panel text uses internal labels ("rung 1a", "A1", "A2'", "premise D <= 5", "T_h").
- F2. Fig. 1's caption describes encodings but not what the reader should see; the figure needs
  "E1b" to be read.
- F3. The main text cites appendix tables for its verdicts (Tables 1, 4, 5, 7, 8): the reader of
  the 9 pages sees no list of the registered tests and their verdicts. A compact main-text table
  built from Table 8's existing values could replace much of the number-dense prose of Sec. 7.

### 2.5 Citations (W5, W10)

- R1. Sentences where a citation backs a step of our own argument rather than saying what the
  cited work shows, e.g. "the step is close to eta sign (Kunstner et al., 2024; Sabanayagam &
