## 4. Recommended structure (a recommendation; `paper-repo` decides)

The argument the evidence supports, in the order a reader needs it: near the collapsed solution
collapse is an attractor and survival a race against the predictor's decay (theory); the race
predicts held-out linear JEPAs (tests, with failures); it carries to some optimisers, targets and
depths in linear models and not to others (extensions, each with its scope); in a ViT its premise,
that collapse is decided early, fails (scope of the theory). The recommendation, with an
indicative page budget:

1. Introduction (~1.2 pp): the question; the premise; the mechanism in plain words; what is
   tested (held-out, pre-registered, no fitted constants) with two or three headline numbers; how
   far it carries and where it stops, one sentence each; three contributions (theory; held-out
   linear tests and their failures; extensions and the ViT premise test, each with its scope). The
   existing drive-against-decay figure (now Fig. 3, App. A) can serve as the first figure (S4).
2. Related work (~0.6 pp): the static threshold line, Tian et al., De Melo Costa et al. in their own
   sentences; the rest to App. D.
3. Setup (~0.8 pp) and 4. The race (~1.4 pp): as now, with the metric of success (the rank and
   how it is predicted) stated before the experiments.
5. What the heuristics change (~0.5 pp).
6. Held-out tests in linear JEPAs (~1.4 pp with Fig. 1): the rank error defined once; aligned and
   general statistics; masking; where it fails (deep predictor, EMA sign, tanh under Bernoulli
   masks), AdamW pointed to Sec. 7.
7. Beyond the race's assumptions, in linear and two-layer models (~1.8 pp with a summary table of
   the registered tests): one fixed pattern per subsection: what changes in the theory, what was
   predicted and registered, the held-out result against the best baseline, the verdict and its
   scope. AdamW; the normalised target; depth (deep linear encoder, deep predictor); the two-layer
   encoder (gradient descent passes; AdamW and normalised target not validated, hence linear-only
   results); weight-decay schedules.
8. A vision transformer (~0.8 pp with the switch figure): the step-0 rule (now Sec. 6.3), the race
   readout and the switch test (now Sec. 7.5) together: collapse is not decided early there.
9. Discussion and limitations (~0.4 pp): the answer to the title question, what is and is not
   supported, and why no design is recommended.

Fig. 2's panels would then sit with their sections (F1); the appendices follow the main text's
order (S6) and keep the registration identifiers as provenance, with a glossary to the main
text's names (T1).

## 5. Writing standards the revision is checked against

1. Each paragraph opens with its claim; numbers follow as support, at most two or three per
   sentence; the rest goes to a table or the appendix.
2. Every quantity is defined at first use (metric, interval, n); every result states its status
   (registered and its verdict, post hoc, retrospective, development, theory) the same way
   throughout.
3. No internal label in the main text; one name per baseline across text, figures and tables.
4. Every figure and table is cited in the text before or beside it with what to see; captions say
   what is plotted, n and the statistic, and the claim; no internal labels in panels.
5. Every citation sits in a sentence that says what the cited work shows; our own steps are
   stated as ours.
6. Every claim matches A-claims' verdict; every change in a claim's strength is in the change
   record with its evidence.
7. Main text within 9 pages; references and appendices after it; ICLR 2027 template unmodified;
   venue checker passes; no work after 18 Oct 2026.

